The ''' [[2017–18 in Scottish football|2017–18 season]]''' was the 129th season of competitive football by [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]. They competed in the [[2017–18 Scottish Premiership|Scottish Premiership]], [[2017–18 Scottish League Cup|League Cup]], [[2017–18 Scottish Cup|Scottish Cup]], [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] and [[2017–18 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]]. Celtic won all three domestic tournaments, completing a [[Treble (association football)|double treble]].

==Background==
The [[2016–17 Celtic F.C. season|previous season]] saw Celtic win the domestic treble, remaining undefeated domestically, recording only four draws in the [[Scottish Premiership]]. The squad reported back for pre-season training on 19 June, following a short break after the [[2017 Scottish Cup Final|Scottish Cup Final]] on 27 May.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://twitter.com/celticfc/status/876741704036093957 | title=First Day Back | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | work=twitter.com | date=19 June 2017 | access-date=3 July 2017 | archive-date=2 December 2020 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201202220056/https://pbs.twimg.com/hashflag/config-2020-12-02-22.json | url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic assistant manager [[Chris Davies (football coach)|Chris Davies]] commented:

<blockquote style="border-style: solid; border:0px; border-left: thick solid green; margin-left:20px;">''"It's a little bit different in England where you can have six or seven, or sometimes even eight weeks away. Some players have had the three weeks' rest after the cup final while others have had 10 days and some possibly even less. So we've managed that in terms of physical conditioning to make sure that they are in the best place possible. But we have noticed straight away that they've come back in really good condition and that is linked to not having such a long break. So we're happy with that, they are nice and fresh and have great enthusiasm and energy for the new season, so that's all good."''<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12831 | title=Davies: Short break helped the summer fitness levels of the Hoops | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | work=celticfc.net | date=26 June 2017 | access-date=3 July 2017 | archive-date=15 December 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181215122601/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12831 | url-status=live }}</ref></blockquote>

On 2 July 2017, Celtic announced that [[Scott Brown (footballer, born June 1985)|Scott Brown]] would receive a testimonial for ten years of service to the club.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12863 | title=Scott Brown Testimonial Year | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | work=celticfc.net | date=2 July 2017 | access-date=25 July 2017 | archive-date=5 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170705122128/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12863 | url-status=live }}</ref>

==Pre-season and friendlies==
Celtic preceded the 2017–18 campaign with a pre-season tour of Austria, with matches against [[FC Blau-Weiß Linz|Blau-Weiß Linz]] and [[SK Rapid Wien|Rapid Wien]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12757 | title=Pre-season friendlies in Austria as Hoops head to training camp | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=6 June 2017 | access-date=1 July 2017 | archive-date=3 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170703084212/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12757 | url-status=live }}</ref> The Hoops then travelled to the [[Czech Republic]] to face [[Slavia Prague]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12776 | title=Celtic to play Slavia Prague in pre-season friendly | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=12 June 2017 | access-date=1 July 2017 | archive-date=15 June 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170615192529/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12776 | url-status=live }}</ref> which was followed by a trip to [[Republic of Ireland|Ireland]] to play [[Shamrock Rovers]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12738 | title=Hoops united as manager takes the treble-winners to Ireland | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=1 June 2017 | access-date=1 July 2017 | archive-date=5 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170705224544/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12738 | url-status=live }}</ref> The pre-season schedule also included games against [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]]<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12851 | title=Hoops announce friendly against Lyon at Celtic Park | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=30 June 2017 | access-date=1 July 2017 | archive-date=3 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170703025156/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12851 | url-status=live }}</ref> and [[Sunderland A.F.C.|Sunderland]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12844 | title=Celts travel to play Sunderland for the Dafabet Cup | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=29 June 2017 | access-date=1 July 2017 | archive-date=3 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170703163810/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12844 | url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic recorded their first pre-season victory against Blau-Weiß Linz. [[Brendan Rodgers]] fielded a different team in each half, with a cameo appearance from his son, [[Anton Rodgers (footballer)|Anton]], during the second period.<ref>{{cite web|title=Why Celtic boss Brendan Rodgers played his son Anton in friendly win over BW Linz|url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-boss-brendan-rodgers-explains-10705802|website=dailyrecord.co.uk|publisher=Daily Record|access-date=2 July 2017|archive-date=1 July 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170701024818/http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-boss-brendan-rodgers-explains-10705802|url-status=live}}</ref> The match played out in typical pre-season fashion, until [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] scored a late winner.<ref>{{cite web|title=BW Linz 0 Celtic 1 as James Forrest cracks home late winner and Anton Rodgers features for Hoops|url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-match-reports/bw-linz-0-celtic-1-10705357|website=dailyrecord.co.uk|publisher=Daily Record|access-date=2 July 2017|archive-date=1 July 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170701000316/http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-match-reports/bw-linz-0-celtic-1-10705357|url-status=live}}</ref> Celtic's next opponents – Rapid Vienna of the [[Austrian Football Bundesliga|Austrian Bundesliga]] – marked a step up in quality. Rapid took the lead on the stroke of half time, but this was later cancelled out by a [[Moussa Dembélé (French footballer)|Moussa Dembélé]] penalty, which earned Celtic a 1–1 draw. Following the match, Brendan Rodgers expressed his dissatisfaction with the first half performance, but praised the second half display and the progress made so far in pre-season.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12861 | title=Manager hails Celtic's resolve after Rapid Vienna test | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=2 July 2017 | access-date=30 July 2017 | archive-date=30 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170730235955/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12861 | url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic continued their preparations for the new season with a game against Slavia Prague. The match ended goalless, with [[Dedryck Boyata]] forced off through injury at the end of the first half. The Celtic manager was pleased with his team's efforts, commenting that the Czech champions provided an excellent test in the build-up to the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League|UEFA Champions League]] qualifiers.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12871 | title=Brendan Rodgers satisfied after Celts draw with Slavia | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=4 July 2017 | access-date=5 July 2017 | archive-date=10 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170710132914/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12871 | url-status=live }}</ref> Shamrock Rovers provided the opposition in Celtic's final match before the competitive action resumed. The Bhoys strolled to a 9–0 victory, with seven players on the scoresheet, including a first goal for [[Jonny Hayes]]. Brendan Rodgers was again delighted with the level of performance produced by his players.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12891 | title=Manager hails free-scoring Celts after Dublin victory | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=8 July 2017 | access-date=8 July 2017 | archive-date=11 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170711160838/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12891 | url-status=live }}</ref> The day after Celtic's first European qualifier, a much-changed side was soundly beaten 4–0 by Lyon of [[Ligue 1]]. Celtic finished the match with nine academy players on the pitch.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12925 | title=Celts lose to clinical Lyon in pre-season friendly | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=15 July 2017 | access-date=29 July 2017 | archive-date=29 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170729181156/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12925 | url-status=live }}</ref> The Hoops rounded off pre-season with a game against Sunderland, recently relegated from the [[Premier League]]. As both clubs share [[Dafabet]] as their main sponsor, a one-off trophy was on offer for the winner. Celtic comfortably defeated the [[EFL Championship|Championship]] side 5–0, with [[Callum McGregor]] scoring a hat-trick. Brendan Rodgers praised the large travelling support of 9,000 and commented that the result would boost confidence in the squad ahead of the next European qualifier.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12989 | title=Manager: Bhoys were outstanding in emphatic Sunderland success | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=29 July 2017 | access-date=30 July 2017 | archive-date=31 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170731001915/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12989 | url-status=live }}</ref>
