====Third qualifying round====
On 19 July, it was determined that Celtic would face [[Rosenborg BK|Rosenborg]] (Norway) in the Third Qualifying Round of the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League|UEFA Champions League]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/12940 | title=Manager hails stylish Celts after resounding European win | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=19 July 2017 | access-date=29 July 2017 | archive-date=29 July 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170729173613/http://www.celticfc.net/news/12940 | url-status=live }}</ref> The Norwegian champions overcame [[Dundalk F.C.|Dundalk]] to set up their first meeting with the Scottish champions since the [[2001–02 UEFA Champions League]] group stage. In October 2001, an [[Alan Thompson (footballer, born 1973)|Alan Thompson]] free-kick secured a [[2001–02 UEFA Champions League group stage#Celtic v Rosenborg|1–0]] victory for Celtic in Glasgow. Later that month, [[Martin O'Neill]]'s team succumbed to a [[2001–02 UEFA Champions League group stage#Rosenborg v Celtic|2–0]] defeat in [[Trondheim]], with former Celtic player [[Harald Brattbakk]] scoring both goals. Brendan Rodgers' side were forced to play without a recognised striker in the first leg, which finished goalless. Injuries to [[Moussa Dembélé (French footballer)|Moussa Dembélé]] and [[Leigh Griffiths]] – the latter was also suspended – meant that [[Tom Rogic]] was tasked with deputising in a [[Forward (association football)#False 9|False 9]] role. The return leg saw [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] start in the same position and score the deciding goal to seal Celtic's place in the next round. The result secured European football for the club until the end of the year.

